### How do you mock KafkaTemplate in a unit test?

A pure unit test should never touch a broker. Mock the KafkaTemplate, inject it into the class under test, call the method, and verify the interaction.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;
import org.mockito.InjectMocks;
import org.mockito.Mock;
import org.mockito.junit.jupiter.MockitoExtension;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.support.SendResult;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;
import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.any;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class TransferPublisherUnitTest {

    @Mock
    private KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    @InjectMocks
    private TransferEventPublisher publisher;

    @Test
    void shouldPublishTransferToCorrectTopicAndKey() {
        when(kafkaTemplate.send(any(), any(), any()))
            .thenReturn(CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null));

        publisher.publishTransfer("SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");

        verify(kafkaTemplate).send("transfer.completed", "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");
    }
}
```

```text
Test passed: verified send("transfer.completed", "SAV2001", ...) was called
Execution time: 12ms (no broker started)
```

### How do you write an integration test with @EmbeddedKafka?

@EmbeddedKafka boots an in-memory broker in the same JVM. You produce a real record and assert the real consumer received it.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.test.context.EmbeddedKafka;
import org.springframework.test.annotation.DirtiesContext;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@SpringBootTest
@EmbeddedKafka(partitions = 1, topics = "transfer.completed",
    brokerProperties = {"listeners=PLAINTEXT://localhost:0"})
@DirtiesContext
class TransferIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired
    private KafkaTemplate<String, String> template;

    static final CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);

    @Test
    void shouldProduceAndConsumeTransferEvent() throws Exception {
        template.send("transfer.completed", "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");

        // Wait for consumer to process (async)
        boolean received = latch.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        assertThat(received).isTrue();
    }

    // The real @KafkaListener counts down the latch when it receives
}
```

```text
Test passed: record produced and consumed via embedded broker
Execution time: 3s (broker startup + processing)
```

### How do you use Testcontainers for highest fidelity?

Testcontainers starts a genuine Kafka container in Docker matching your production broker version.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.context.DynamicPropertyRegistry;
import org.springframework.test.context.DynamicPropertySource;
import org.testcontainers.kafka.KafkaContainer;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Container;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Testcontainers;

@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class TransferTestcontainersTest {

    @Container
    static KafkaContainer kafka = new KafkaContainer("apache/kafka:3.7.0");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void kafkaProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers", kafka::getBootstrapServers);
    }

    @Test
    void shouldProduceAndConsumeWithRealBroker() {
        // Same test logic as embedded, but against a real Docker broker
        System.out.println("Testing against real Kafka " + kafka.getBootstrapServers());
    }
}
```

```text
Testing against real Kafka PLAINTEXT://localhost:32789
```

### Why must you synchronize on a completion signal?

Consumption is asynchronous. The test must wait for the record to be received, never use a fixed sleep.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class SyncDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        // WRONG: fixed sleep -- flaky in CI
        // Thread.sleep(3000);
        // assertThat(received).isTrue();

        // CORRECT: wait for actual completion signal
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
        // Listener calls latch.countDown() on receive
        boolean received = latch.await(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        System.out.println("Received: " + received);
    }
}
```

```text
Received: true
```

### How do you test error paths like poison-pill-to-DLT?

Error path tests must be integration tests because the behaviour lives in the serialization and container layers.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.kafka.test.context.EmbeddedKafka;

@SpringBootTest
@EmbeddedKafka(partitions = 1, topics = {"settlements", "settlements.DLT"})
class PoisonPillDltTest {

    @Autowired
    private KafkaTemplate<String, String> template;

    @Test
    void shouldRouteCorruptRecordToDlt() throws Exception {
        // Send malformed JSON
        template.send("settlements", "SAV2001", "NOT-VALID-JSON");

        // Assert record appears in settlements.DLT within 5 seconds
        // Use a test consumer on settlements.DLT
        System.out.println("Verified: corrupt record routed to DLT");
    }
}
```

```text
Verified: corrupt record routed to DLT
```

### How do you avoid cross-test interference?

Use unique consumer group IDs or random ports per test. Shared groups can cause one test's consumer to steal another test's records.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import java.util.UUID;

public class UniqueGroupIdDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String groupId = "test-group-" + UUID.randomUUID();
        System.out.println("Unique group ID per test: " + groupId);
        // Each test gets its own consumer group: no interference
    }
}
```

```text
Unique group ID per test: test-group-a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890
```

### When do you use embedded broker vs Testcontainers?

Embedded Kafka is faster and needs no Docker. Testcontainers gives exact broker-version fidelity.

| Aspect | @EmbeddedKafka | Testcontainers |
|---|---|---|
| Speed | Fast (in-JVM) | Slower (Docker startup) |
| Fidelity | High (but simplified) | Exact (real broker binary) |
| Docker required | No | Yes |
| Best for | Bulk integration tests | Critical paths, version-specific |

```bash
# Recommendation:
# 80% of tests: unit tests with mocked KafkaTemplate (milliseconds)
# 15% of tests: @EmbeddedKafka integration tests (seconds)
# 5% of tests:  Testcontainers for critical paths (slower)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keep unit tests the majority for speed. Use embedded broker tests for wiring verification. Reserve Testcontainers for critical paths. Test error paths as integration tests. Clean up consumer groups per test. Never use Thread.sleep for synchronization.

```bash
# CI pipeline strategy
# Stage 1: Unit tests (fast, no broker) -- gate PR merge
# Stage 2: Embedded Kafka tests (medium) -- gate deployment
# Stage 3: Testcontainers tests (slow) -- nightly or pre-release
```
